{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "והבשר תאכל", :translit "u-vasar tokhal", :gloss "and the flesh you shall eat"} {:he "שמור ושמעת", :translit "shmor ve-shamata", :gloss "observe and you shall hear"} {:he "ע\"ה", :translit "am ha-aretz", :gloss "one of the land (ignoramus)"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut: adjacent verse segments cited to derive a legal inference"}], :frame {:text "The hermeneutical principle of semikhat parshiyot derives law from the juxtaposition of adjacent verses or phrases; here the proximity of \"and you shall eat the flesh\" (Deuteronomy 12:27) to the language of observance (Deuteronomy 12:28) yields a restriction. The halakhah that an am ha-aretz is forbidden to eat meat is addressed at Pesachim 49b, where it is stated that an am ha-aretz is forbidden to eat meat.", :sources ["Pesachim 49b" "Deuteronomy 12:27" "Deuteronomy 12:28"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut: adjacent verse segments cited to derive a legal inference", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:36:18.056174727Z", :model "glm-5.2"}